;; A Raspberry Pi 5 as the way into your own network.
;;
;; The same thing raspberrypi3-64-tailscale is, on a board that boots. The Pi
;; 3 build of that image reached the firmware's rainbow test pattern and no
;; further - the card was right, the board was fine, the kernel never started
;; - which is a diagnosis for another day and not one worth having on the day
;; the garage door is meant to work.
;;
;; It joins the tailnet at boot and advertises the LAN it sits on, so an
;; ESPHome relay is reachable from a phone with nothing installed on the
;; relay and nothing forwarded on the router.
;;
;; Set TS_AUTHKEY and TS_ROUTES in /etc/default/tailscale, approve the route
;; once in the admin console, and turn off key expiry for the node or it
;; drops off the tailnet in a few months and the door stops answering.
;;
;; A /32 for the one device you want reached is tighter than the whole /24,
;; and mDNS does not cross the tunnel, so use the address rather than a
;; .local name.
;;
;; No appliance daemons: a route into a network and a simulated device have
;; no reason to share an image.

(image raspberrypi5-tailscale
  (compose
    image:raspberrypi5
    feature:dev-ssh
    feature:tailscale)

  ;; tailscaled is a Go binary carrying its own WireGuard, DNS handling and
  ;; network stack, and its state directory grows.
  (override
    (value buildroot:BR2_TARGET_ROOTFS_EXT2_SIZE "512M"))

  (verified-against (buildroot "2025.02.16")))
